{
  /* The MCP server, compiled on its own and OPTIONAL.

     It is a separate program rather than five more files in `tsconfig.json`'s `include` because it
     is the one part of this package a consumer may want nothing to do with. A mobile app installs
     this package to edit video; it has no use for a tool server, and `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk`
     pulls in around 190 packages - a web framework, a CORS layer, a JOSE implementation - none of
     which belong anywhere near an app bundle. Kept out of the plugin build, none of it is reachable
     from anything an app imports, and `scripts/build-mcp.mjs` will not run this compile at all
     unless the SDK is installed or the build is explicitly told to.

     `src/mcp` imports the editor core out of `src/editor`, so the common root is `src` and the
     emitted tree mirrors it: the server lands at `mcp/mcp/` with `mcp/editor/` and `mcp/data/`
     beside it. That doubling is the price of the tree being SELF-CONTAINED, which is what matters
     here - `mcp/` can be deleted whole, and the plugin build neither produces it nor depends on it.

     ESM only. There is no CommonJS half because there is no consumer that needs one: this is a
     program Node runs and a module a Node host imports, and both have read ESM for years. */
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "outDir": "mcp",
    "rootDir": "src"
  },
  "include": ["src/mcp/**/*.ts"],
  "exclude": ["src/**/*.test.ts"]
}
